<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.ActionBar"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False">

    <!--
        WHAT SOMEBODY CAN DO TO THE ROWS THEY PICKED, where they can see it. Arriving 2026-08-25,
        backlog 224, and the thing worth saying first is how it was found: a report written from
        four screenshots by somebody who had never seen this project put it first on a list of ten,
        and a search of `docs/01`, `docs/04` and `docs/11` for a bar of this kind came back empty.
        It had never been proposed here, so it had never been refused - the window has been able to
        change a machine since 2026-08-19 and the only way in was the right mouse button.

        EVERY BUTTON HERE OPENS A PLAN AND NOTHING ELSE, which is rule 1 of the project notes and
        not a temporary arrangement. The words carry it: each one ends in the three dots Windows
        has used since the eighties to mean "this asks before it acts", and the tooltip says it in
        a sentence. What actually changes a machine is one button, it names the act - "Stop Windows Search" - and it
        lives at the foot of the plan.

        WHY NOT THE MENU WORDING. Under the right button these are questions - "What stopping would
        do" - which is right there and wrong here: a bar of four questions reads as a quiz, and a
        toolbar has room for a verb rather than a clause. The three dots carry the same promise in
        the space a button has.

        WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE, said rather than left to be noticed. The column picker stays
        at the end of the filters row: it changes what the list SHOWS, and everything here changes
        what the machine DOES. Putting them together would be the tidier bar and the worse one.

        Focusable and IsTabStop are off because a UserControl IS a ContentControl and a
        ContentControl is BOTH BY DEFAULT - measured, not assumed. Without them this row is a tab
        stop that does nothing, in front of the buttons it holds. `docs/10` trap 8.
    -->

    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
        <Button x:Name="StopButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="actionStop"
                Content="{DynamicResource gui.action.stop}"
                Style="{StaticResource WriteAction}"
                Click="StopAsked" />

        <Button x:Name="StartButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="actionStart"
                Content="{DynamicResource gui.action.start}"
                Style="{StaticResource WriteAction}"
                Click="StartAsked" />

        <Button x:Name="RestartButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="actionRestart"
                Content="{DynamicResource gui.action.restart}"
                Style="{StaticResource WriteAction}"
                Click="RestartAsked" />

        <!--
            THE TWO VERBS THAT CAN END A PROCESS, arriving 2026-09-16 - backlog 374, the owner's
            decision, and the second on the owner's word that a window with the verb has the whole
            verb. Until this line the window offered them only under a stop or a restart that had
            already failed, so an administrator who KNEW the entry was hung had to ask politely,
            wait the whole ceiling, and then be offered what they came for.

            THE SAME PLAN AS `bws kill NAME` AND AS THE OFFER: a polite stop first, and the process
            ended only if that does not arrive - both steps in the preview, the second marked as the
            one standing behind the first. Three dots like the three above them: each opens a plan
            and nothing else, and what changes the machine is still the one button at the foot of
            the sheet, which by then can name the process by number.

            LAST OF THE MOVEMENT VERBS RATHER THAN NEXT TO STOP, and that is the order the analysis
            argues: the road to forcing runs through the ordinary stop, and a bar that put Stop and
            Force stop side by side would read as a pair to choose between. Before the startup type
            because that one changes configuration rather than moving anything - the line the bar
            already draws between its verbs.

            ONE ENTRY AT A TIME, and the reason is written at length in `docs/ANALIZA-FORCE` 15.6:
            two entries sharing one process would be two plans ending the same process, which the
            bulk plan tells apart by service name and cannot see. Live only when the selection
            stands for exactly one entry - a folded per-user family stands for many - and the
            reason a button is off is on the button, like every other refusal in this bar. The
            refusal itself lives in the window's Preview, where the row menu arrives too.

            ORDINARY BUTTONS RATHER THAN THE RED OUTLINE THE OFFER WEARS, and that is a decision
            rather than an omission. On the sheet the red outline answers a red sentence - it is the
            way out of a failure already on screen. In this bar nothing is red, so a red outline here
            would make the verbs that can lose data the loudest things above the list, which is the
            opposite of what a rarely used destructive verb should be. The word carries it, the dots
            keep the promise, and the sheet it opens says "end the process behind" in its steps -
            WCAG 1.4.1, `docs/11` 3.1.
        -->
        <Button x:Name="ForceStopButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="actionForceStop"
                Content="{DynamicResource gui.action.forceStop}"
                Style="{StaticResource WriteAction}"
                Click="ForceStopAsked" />

        <!--
            THE SAME, AND THEN BRING IT BACK. Apart from the button above for the reason the core
            keeps ForceRestart apart from ForceStop: what has to be put back is decided when the
            plan is built, the entries that shared the process included, and a person choosing
            between the two should see two words rather than a switch.
        -->
        <Button x:Name="ForceRestartButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="actionForceRestart"
                Content="{DynamicResource gui.action.forceRestart}"
                Style="{StaticResource WriteAction}"
                Click="ForceRestartAsked" />

        <!--
            WHAT THE MANAGER DOES WITH THE ENTRY AT BOOT, arriving 2026-08-25. A menu rather than
            four buttons, for the reason the column picker won the same argument: three more buttons
            in a bar of four is a bar nobody scans, and the theme already dresses a menu.

            THREE TYPES AND NOT FIVE, and the two missing ones are a decision. Boot and System belong
            to drivers, which this tool refuses to operate on at all - offering them would be a menu
            item that always ends in a refusal. Automatic-delayed is a separate flag rather than a
            start type, so setting it is a different call and a later slice.

            Three dots like the three above it: this opens a plan, and what changes the machine is
            still the one button at the foot of it.
        -->
        <Button x:Name="StartTypeButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="actionStartType"
                Content="{DynamicResource gui.action.startType}"
                Style="{StaticResource WriteAction}"
                Click="StartTypeAsked">
            <Button.ContextMenu>
                <ContextMenu>
                    <MenuItem x:Name="AutomaticItem"
                              Header="{DynamicResource gui.cell.start.automatic}"
                              Click="SetAutomatic" />
                    <MenuItem x:Name="ManualItem"
                              Header="{DynamicResource gui.cell.start.manual}"
                              Click="SetManual" />
                    <MenuItem x:Name="DisabledItem"
                              Header="{DynamicResource gui.cell.start.disabled}"
                              Click="SetDisabled" />
                </ContextMenu>
            </Button.ContextMenu>
        </Button>

        <!--
            The line between what is done to a service and what is done to the listing. A shape
            rather than a gap, because a gap at this width reads as an accident.
        -->
        <Rectangle Width="1"
                   Fill="{StaticResource SurfaceRowLine}"
                   Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}" />

        <!--
            READING THE MACHINE AGAIN, and it is not one of the three above it: nothing it does
            reaches a service. It is here because F5 is a key nobody was told about, which is the
            same argument the column picker won on `docs/11` section 6 - a feature reachable only
            by a key you already know is a feature you do not have.
        -->
        <Button x:Name="RefreshButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="actionRefresh"
                Content="{DynamicResource gui.action.refresh}"
                ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.action.refresh.hint}"
                Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                Click="RefreshAsked" />

        <!--
            WHAT IS ON SCREEN, AS A FILE, and it belongs on this side of the line for the same
            reason Refresh does: nothing it does reaches a service. Three dots again, because it
            asks where to put the file before it writes one.

            Never off. It exports what the list SHOWS rather than what somebody picked, so a
            selection of nothing is the ordinary case rather than a reason to refuse.
        -->
        <Button x:Name="ExportButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="actionExport"
                Content="{DynamicResource gui.action.export}"
                ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.action.export.hint}"
                Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                Click="ExportAsked" />

        <!--
            WHAT THIS MACHINE LOOKS LIKE, `G`, and it belongs on this side of the line with the
            other two: nothing it does reaches a service.

            THE SPECIFICATION DOES NOT ASK FOR THIS BUTTON AND THE SCREEN NEEDS IT - owner's
            decision, 2026-08-25. `G` describes the overview as something shown at a first run and
            says nothing about getting back to it, and the preferences file is written the first
            time somebody puts it away - so without this the screen would appear once in the life of
            a profile. The person it is written for is an administrator opening this on their
            twentieth unknown server, who wants it every time.

            No three dots, because it opens nothing and asks nothing - it changes what the middle of
            the window is showing, which is the same promise Refresh makes about the same area.
        -->
        <Button x:Name="OverviewButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="actionOverview"
                Content="{DynamicResource gui.overview.button}"
                ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.overview.button.hint}"
                Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                Click="OverviewAsked" />
    </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
